  "fields": {
    "control_architecture": {
      "value": "external_feedback",
      "claim": "The control architecture is external feedback because the propulsion direction is controlled via an external magnetic field and propulsion strength through light intensity, both modulated by an Arduino.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "By leveraging both light and magnetic fields, we achieve tunable propulsion strength through light intensity, while the propulsion direction is precisely controlled via an external magnetic field, which exploits the weak ferromagnetic nature of the hematite cube to regulate particle orientation."
        },
        {
          "quote": "The motion of the particles was controlled by independently tuning their propulsion speed and orientation. For hematite cube-embedded TPM particles, the protruding hematite cube serves as a photocatalyst, decomposing hydrogen peroxide upon UV illumination. This reaction generates a local chemical concentration gradient of constituent molecules, driving self-diffusiophoretic propulsion. The particles were propelled along their symmetry axis, with motion directed toward the hematite cube. Their speed was modulated by varying the UV light intensity, which was controlled via a UV LED driver (Solis® LED Driver) using an external transistor-transistor-logic (TTL) signal. By adjusting the duty cycle of the TTL signal via an Arduino, the current through the LED driver was regulated, thereby tuning the UV intensity. The UV light could be set to a constant level or varied dynamically over time. The orientation of the particles was controlled via the weak intrinsic magnetic moment of the hematite cube, which aligned with an externally applied magnetic field. To generate and control this field, a permanent magnet was mounted on the rotor of a servo motor (Parallel Feedback 360° High-Speed Servo), positioned 5 cm above the sample. The magnet’s north-south axis was oriented parallel to the substrate and perpendicular to the motor’s rotation axis, which was centered over the sample using a screw gauge to minimize drift. When the magnetic field was rotated, the cube followed synchronously (with a field strength of approximately 1–2 mT in the sample plane), enabling precise control over its orientation. The rotation speed and direction of the magnetic field were controlled using pulse width modulation (PWM). The motor allowed for tunable angular velocities in both clockwise and counterclockwise directions, with rotation speeds of 0.2–3 Hz. An Arduino Uno R3 microcontroller with a PWM shield (Adafruit 16-Channel PWM/Servo Shield) was used to provide the TTL signal and the PWM signal, which controls the propulsion strength and direction, respectively."
